  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and Rationale**\nWe transform the discrete optimization problem for nearly periodic functions on \\([0,2]\\) into a high-amplitude Fourier optimization problem. The core idea is to represent the solution via a Fourier series truncated at \\(N_{\\text{max}} = 7\\)**, yielding a smooth function that respects periodicity. This approach is selected because:\n- **Direct representation of shifts**: Circular correlation becomes equivalent to linear comparison, eliminating expensive convolution during scoring.\n- **Constraint enforcement**: The \\(\\int h(x) dx = 1\\) condition matches the real Fourier component symmetry, requiring no re-projection.\n- **Escaping local optima**: Entropy-based gradients with adaptive clipping better explore distances between periodic sequences of overlapping ranges.\n- **Runtime reduction**: Fourier operations are an FFT, so operations scale as \\(\\mathcal{O}(N \\log N)\\) for generation and scoring, versus the cellular convolution routines of current implementations.\n\nThe key limitation is limiting our approximation to \\(N_{\\text{max}}\\). By prior theory (Walsh functions), truncated Fourier series converge geometrically for bandlimited functions from \\(L^2[0,2]\\), and the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem is such a bandlimited case. We represent \\(h(x)\\) as:\n\n\\[ h(x) = \\text{Re} \\left( \\sum_{k=0}^{7} c_k e^{2\\pi i k x} \\right) \\]\n\nwith constraints:\n- \\(c_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^+\\) (forcing positive bias)\n- \\(|c_1| = |c_7|\\), \\(|c_2| = |c_6|\\), and \\(|c_3| = |c_5|\\) for conjugacy (periodicity)\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification**\n**Variables**: Complex coefficients \\(c_0\\) (real) and \\(c_k\\) (complex for \\(k=1,\\ldots,7\\)).\n\n**Objective**:\n\\[ \\min_{c \\in \\mathbb{C}^{13}} \\max_{k=-1998}^{1998} \\text{correlation}[h][k] \\times \\frac{2}{600} \\]\n\n**Constraints**:\n1. **Normalization**:\n   \\[ \\frac{2}{600} \\sum_{x \\in \\{0, \\delta x, 2\\delta x\\}} h(x) \\approx 1 \\]\n   enforced via real-part symmetry (\\(c_0, \\text{Re}(c_1), \\ldots, \\text{Re}(c_7) > 0\\) post-projection.\n\n2. **Domain constraint**:\n   \\[ \\forall x \\in [0,2]: \\quad 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1 \\]\n   where \\( h(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\text{Re}\\{c_k e^{2\\pi i k x}\\} \\)\n\n**Implementation**: \n- Use `numpy.fft.irfft` to reconstruct grid points from complex Fourier coefficients.\n- Fourier normalization: \\(c_k = C_k / (n_{\\text{base}} \\cdot \\delta_{\\text{real}})\\) (where \\(n_{\\text{base}} = 600\\)) to match grid integral.\n- Scoring: Compute the (linear) cross-correlation of the reconstructed real grid \\(h\\) with \\(1-h\\).\n\n**Mathematical relation**: This method naturally models the circular structure of the overlap integral. The score is equivalent to maximizing \\( \\| h \\cdot (1 - h_{\\text{shift}}) \\|\\) over shifts, but through periodic continuation the circular convolution becomes equivalent to linear.\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure**\nThe phases are:\n1. **Input normalization**: Convert parent solution (or initial) to Fourier space (phase 1).\n2. **Entropy-informed gradient descent** with projection (phase 2\u20136).\n3. **Grid reconstruction** (phase 7).\n\n**Phase 1: Conversion to Fourier space**:\n```python\ndef initial_to_fourier(h_grid):\n    n_base = len(h_grid)\n    # DCT to enforce symmetry for projection\n    coeffs = np.fft.rfft(h_grid)\n    # Keep only real part to avoid imaginary leakage\n    coeffs_imag = coeffs[1:].imag # We set imaginary parts arbitrarily to zero\n    coeffs = np.concatenate([np.real(coeffs), coeffs_imag**2 + 1e-10])\n    return coeffs  # Shape (14,)\n```\n\n**Phase 2\u20136: Projected gradient descent via Sinkhorn relaxation** (see code below).\n\n**Core Step**: For each iteration, convert Fourier coefficients to grid points, compute the overlap score via `compute_c5`, apply:\n- **Projection**: \\(\\mathcal{P}(h) = \\text{proj}_{\\text{simplex}} + \\lambda \\nabla J\\)\n  - Sinkhorn projection algorithm (see cutting-plane method) on \\(h \\in [0,1]\\).\n  - Compute gradient: \\(\\nabla J = -\\text{correlation}[h][k] \\text{ for all } k\\)\n\n**Phase 7: Conversion to grid**:\n```python\ndef fourier_to_initial(coeffs, n_target=600):\n    # Convert coeffs back to grid using frequency domain\n    # Replaces initial_h_values with new grid\n    # Actual conversion via inverse FFT\n    h_grid = np.fft.irfft(coeffs * n_target / 600)\n    h_grid = np.clip(h_grid, 0, 1)\n    # Reconstruction re-normalization\n    return h_grid[:n_target]\n```\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Useful Variations**\n**Fourier truncation**: Began with \\(N_{\\text{max}} = 7\\) but has a parameter \\(N_{\\text{max}}\\) that can be increased if clarity wins over performance (\\(9\\) variables tested, but \\(7\\) is enough for \\(600\\) points).\n\n**Gradient smoothing**: Use \\(\\epsilon = 1e-3\\) for Sinkhorn algorithm. Tolerance \\(\\tau = 5e-4\\).\n\n**Gradient descent parameters**:\n\n- Learning rate schedule: Optimized via exponential decay: initial rate \\(lr = 0.2\\) decays by 0.1 every \\(1000\\) iterations.\n- Weight decay: \\(1e-3\\).\n- Max iterations: \\(50000\\).\n- Early termination: If max score plateau lasts \\(100\\) iterations.\n\n**Strategies**:\n\n- **Initialisation by parent**: Convert parent solution to Fourier space with noise perturbation (\\(\\sigma \\approx 1e-4\\)).\n- **Regularization**: Use Dirichlet regularization (\\(\\alpha \\approx 0.002\\)) during initialization to eliminate pathological frequency content.\n- **Restart**: Reset gradient descent \\(5\\) times in each phase upon threshold plateaus.\n\n**Computing budget**:\n- \\(N_{\\text{max}}\\)-limited complexity \\(\\sim (N_{\\text{max}} \\log N_{\\text{max}})\\) per iteration.\n- Total operations: \\(50,000 \\times N_{\\text{max}} \\log N_{\\text{max}} \\approx 5 \\times 10^6\\) operations.\n\n**Implementation checks**:\n\n- \\(0 \\leq h \\leq 1\\): Enforced by projecting the grid via clipping and then Fourier projection.\n- \\(\\sum h = n_{\\text{base}}/2\\): Enforced by the zeroth Fourier component symmetry.\n\n### 5. Computational Budget and Robustness**\n**Estimated time**: \\(70\u201380\\%\\) of budget used by phase \\(2\u20136\\). Final time up to \\(600\\) seconds.\n\n**Stop conditions**:\n- No improvement for \\(500\\) iterations.\n- Non-finite gradient detected.\n- Time budget exhausted.\n\n**Recovery measures**:\n\n- Use the most recently improved solution if descent gets stuck.\n- When constraints violated silently (by Sinkhorn projection crash), default to grid clipping.\n- Fallback to parent solution (if available).\n\n### 6. Validation and Return Contract**\n**Sanity checks**:\n\n- \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\): Global clipping in conversion.\n- Sum condition: \\(|\\sum h[i]| / (n/2) \\leq 1e-6\\).\n- Score re-computed using `compute_c5`.\n\n**Return requirements**:\n\n- \\(h \\in [0,1]^n\\), where \\(n\\) is the parent resolution or \\(600\\).\n- \\(C_5\\) score for that \\(h\\).\n\nThe returned solution must be a valid grid representation (via the `np.correlate` scoring function) satisfying the same constraints.",
